= Conclusions

I believe this work, once properly refined, can really be useful in real-world applications.
Once there is a cryptanalytic foundation for supporting better overhead factors efficiently
(@sec:overf), the overhead of using EMVP instead of standard matrix-vector products shrinks
considerably.

Using a TEE also solves the latency issues of having to go to the network layer for each non-linear
operation. In theory, this makes the whole protocol much more valuable for real-world applications.


== Limitations and further work <sec:limit>

=== Field size

My field is implemented over 32 bits. This choice was made to make the GPU implementation more
practical, since GPUs often do not have access to 64-bit precision, although it can often be
simulated.

I did not explore 64-bit-wide prime fields. I believe this would create a trade-off between speed,
quantization precision, and complexity.

=== Further GPU kernel optimization

The GPU kernel for the `Answer` phase can be further improved.

A possible first experiment would be to rewrite it in CUDA, which should be a relatively easy task
with the help of an AI assistant. I chose to skip this part because I lacked suitable hardware on
which to test it and because linking CUDA with Rust is not as straightforward, so I kept to the
simpler approach for this work.

I also believe the kernels can be optimized further by using more advanced techniques, especially
for moving data in and out of the card memory. However, in this moment, I don't have the technical
skills to do any better than this, I believe however that a better result is achievable in practice.

=== Bringing down the server overhead factor <sec:overf>

In my work, I chose to implement the code construction suggested in #citesec(<emvp2025>, "3.1")
without deviating from it. This, as discussed in @sec:emvp-prot, implies $f >= 2$.
I suggest some possible ways to reduce $f$ while keeping encoding through $C$ and $D$ quasi-linear.

/ Vandermonde codes:
  This direction is also hinted by the original authors in their reference implementation
  #citesec(<emvp-code>, "linearcode/LinearCode.go") but is left as a stub.
  This has the technical downside of requiring some NTT trickery such as implementing different
  radix transformations or emulating them with coset-NTT and twisted evaluation.
  Moreover, I could not find any cryptanalytic work supporting this construction.
/ Truncating the cyclic ring code:
  We could even just choose a circulant $M_g$ as in the original construction and only select $k$
  rows from it for the rest of the construction. This also allows for fast encoding and requires
  fewer moving parts than the Vandermonde codes, but its security has also not been verified.

=== Reducing the download size

In the 1D-SLSN construction, each vector is split into $s$ blocks. While this has almost no
performance impact on the computation itself, it increases download size by a factor of $s$, which
combined with the server overhead factor $f$ can be substantial.

The paper suggests #citesec(<emvp2025>, "4.1") that this can be mitigated by using a rate-1 additive
homomorphic encryption mechanism in the protocol. This suggestion was not investigated in my work.
The main reason is that this only matters if the encrypted result has to travel to a remote client.
If we assume that the client is a TEE on the same machine, the AHE only adds overhead for no real
benefit.

=== Quantization

Since EMVP works over a field, regular floating-point numbers should be quantized before entering the
algorithm.
This could be an issue because we want to avoid wraparound during computation.

Luckily, every cloud has a silver lining: since we need to perform a round trip to the client anyway
at every non-linearity or attention block, the client can de-quantize the vector, perform
computations using regular floating-point arithmetic, and re-quantize the result using a different
scale. In theory, this should provide sufficient precision even for a 32-bit field like mine.

=== TEE client

Another research direction would be to implement the client on the same machine as the server, inside
a TEE. This is important because it would allow us to measure the latency of entering and leaving the
trusted environment and assess the actual feasibility of the protocol for real-world use.

An actual user would, for example, send their initial prompt to the LLM provider's TEE, which would
encrypt it, run private autoregression, and return the whole response to the user without requiring
any work from the user's hardware.

